WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.538 --> 00:00:08.323
They have been called "a girl's best friend" and "a symbol of undying love."

2
00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:11.626
They have been a sign of status and wealth for centuries.

3
00:00:12.326 --> 00:00:12.867
What are they?

4
00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:14.448
Diamonds, of course.

5
00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:19.332
But diamonds are not just "a pretty face"; they are a valuable commodity.

6
00:00:20.714 --> 00:00:26.659
Diamonds are a form of carbon, but unlike other forms of carbon such as graphite (the substance used

7
00:00:26.739 --> 00:00:30.927
as pencil lead), diamonds are created in very unusual conditions.

8
00:00:31.908 --> 00:00:40.396
Scientists estimate that diamonds are formed over millennia, 150 to 670 kilometers beneath the earth's surface, where the

9
00:00:40.436 --> 00:00:45.120
extremely high pressure and temperatures needed to crystallize carbon into diamond exist.

10
00:00:46.081 --> 00:00:52.967
Thus, they are extremely old—at minimum, millions of years old, with some as much as three billion years

11
00:00:53.148 --> 00:01:00.079
old. Meteorites can also create diamonds when they slam into the earth's surface, producing in the impact the

12
00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:06.142
necessary temperatures of 1,000 to 1,200 degrees Celsius and extremely high pressure.

13
00:01:06.942 --> 00:01:13.745
Another type of diamond, black or gray opaque stones called carbonados, was created in space and carried to

14
00:01:13.825 --> 00:01:14.966
earth by meteorites.

15
00:01:16.186 --> 00:01:19.468
Diamonds are brought from the depths of the earth to the surface in many ways.

16
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.341
Sometimes, magma carries them upward from deep within the earth's crust.

17
00:01:25.101 --> 00:01:31.763
In certain areas, magma flowing to the surface has formed tunnels, or "pipes," that include a high concentration

18
00:01:31.803 --> 00:01:32.384
of diamonds.

19
00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:39.066
These are commonly called "kimberlite pipes," after the first one discovered in Kimberley, South Africa.

20
00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:46.748
Most diamonds are mined from kimberlite pipes in South Africa, Russia, Australia, Botswana, and Zaire.

21
00:01:47.528 --> 00:01:53.391
Other methods have been created to find diamonds washed into waterways through erosion or landslides.

22
00:01:54.251 --> 00:01:58.333
Diamond mining, unfortunately, brings with it many environmental concerns.

23
00:01:59.013 --> 00:02:05.396
For instance, the chemical leftovers of the mining process are often dumped into rivers and streams, polluting the

24
00:02:05.436 --> 00:02:12.940
water. Another concern within the diamond industry involves conflict diamonds, also known as blood diamonds.

25
00:02:13.756 --> 00:02:17.639
These are diamonds mined and sold to finance wars and violent revolutions.

26
00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:22.081
Blood diamonds are also linked to child slavery and child soldiers.

27
00:02:22.822 --> 00:02:29.206
To make it more difficult to finance war through diamond sales, the diamond industry has organized a certification

28
00:02:29.246 --> 00:02:33.769
system to establish that a diamond has not come from a conflict region.

29
00:02:34.649 --> 00:02:40.732
The system has been largely effective because De Beers, a South African company, controls more than half the

30
00:02:40.772 --> 00:02:46.334
worldwide supply and sales of diamonds and authorizes only a limited number of buyers to acquire diamonds.

31
00:02:47.414 --> 00:02:51.655
Diamonds continue to be popular because of their extraordinary physical properties.

32
00:02:52.456 --> 00:02:59.037
With a rating of ten on the Mohs hardness scale, which measures the relative hardness of minerals, diamonds

33
00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:01.438
are the hardest known naturally occurring substance.

34
00:03:02.378 --> 00:03:05.699
Thus, a diamond can only be scratched by another diamond.

35
00:03:06.499 --> 00:03:11.762
Diamonds also rate highly in brilliance, which refers to how much light a gemstone reflects.

36
00:03:12.643 --> 00:03:18.168
Another property for which diamonds are noted is fire, which refers to the way light disperses into the

37
00:03:18.228 --> 00:03:21.311
full spectrum of colors when it passes through a gemstone.

38
00:03:22.271 --> 00:03:26.595
White light passing through a diamond is separated into all the colors of the rainbow.

39
00:03:27.336 --> 00:03:31.960
Because of these flashes of color, diamonds are said to have a very high degree of fire.

40
00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.864
The greater the brilliance and fire of a particular diamond, the greater its value.

41
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.324
Most people are familiar with clear diamonds, as these are most popular.

42
00:03:43.204 --> 00:03:50.570
Diamonds have many levels of transparency, however, and come in a wide range of colors—including green, blue, red,

43
00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.111
orange, yellow, and brown.

44
00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:57.916
The rarer colored diamonds are known as "fancies" and are more expensive than white diamonds.

45
00:03:58.897 --> 00:04:01.779
Only the highest-quality diamonds are selected for jewelry.

46
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.764
Diamonds are judged according to the "four Cs": color, clarity, carat, and cut.

47
00:04:08.876 --> 00:04:10.797
Color refers to the hue of the diamond.

48
00:04:11.518 --> 00:04:15.761
Colorless diamonds are sought after due to their image of purity and sophistication.

49
00:04:16.621 --> 00:04:20.604
Fancies that exhibit a pure color are highly esteemed and very valuable.

50
00:04:21.504 --> 00:04:26.328
On the other hand, diamonds with tinges of yellow are usually not used as gems at all.

51
00:04:27.068 --> 00:04:30.791
Clarity indicates whether there are internal blemishes or flaws in the stone.

52
00:04:31.711 --> 00:04:36.014
Veins or other visible imperfections will drive down the value of a diamond.

53
00:04:36.855 --> 00:04:38.676
Carat refers to a diamond's mass.

54
00:04:39.468 --> 00:04:41.629
One carat equals two hundred milligrams.

55
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.952
Obviously, the bigger the diamond, the higher its price tag.

56
00:04:46.592 --> 00:04:50.895
Cut has to do with how the diamond is faceted to show off its fire and brilliance.

57
00:04:51.775 --> 00:04:57.058
The cut of a diamond can either enhance its natural beauty or, if poorly done, smother it.

58
00:04:58.159 --> 00:05:03.722
Taking a rough, or uncut, diamond and transforming it into a gemstone is a painstaking process.

59
00:05:04.582 --> 00:05:07.404
A rough diamond can be hard to distinguish from other stones.

60
00:05:08.252 --> 00:05:14.554
Only after the process of cleaving, cutting, and polishing is a diamond transformed into a dazzling gem.

61
00:05:15.494 --> 00:05:20.735
Before a diamond cutter can begin, he or she will carefully examine the stone to see how it

62
00:05:20.755 --> 00:05:23.476
can best be cut to maximize its beauty and value.

63
00:05:24.296 --> 00:05:30.318
It is easier and less wasteful to split the diamond along planes that are naturally weaker, so diamond

64
00:05:30.358 --> 00:05:32.759
cutters first divide the stone along those lines.

65
00:05:33.679 --> 00:05:37.580
Then, following a carefully devised plan, the cutter will make facets.

66
00:05:38.512 --> 00:05:41.815
Often, about half the diamond is cut away during this process.

67
00:05:42.535 --> 00:05:49.420
The most popular design, the "brilliant" cut, usually has 58 facets and allows the stone to reflect the

68
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.841
maximum amount of light.

69
00:05:51.462 --> 00:05:56.585
While the diamonds most people are familiar with are in the form of gems, not all diamonds are

70
00:05:56.625 --> 00:05:57.866
processed into jewelry.

71
00:05:58.727 --> 00:06:02.570
In fact, 80 percent of diamonds are used in industrial processes.

72
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:08.895
Diamonds with flaws in color or shape, or without the brilliance and fire necessary to capture the eye

73
00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:13.278
and sell as gemstones, are employed in a variety of manufacturing sectors.

74
00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:19.743
Some are used as a single crystal, while others are ground into a fine powder and applied to

75
00:06:19.823 --> 00:06:21.585
saw blades and the tips of drills.

76
00:06:22.526 --> 00:06:27.970
Because diamonds are so hard, they can cut, scratch, scrape, and drill through all other surfaces.

77
00:06:28.891 --> 00:06:34.795
Diamonds are used not only in mining, but also in the oil and gas industries, electronics, and even

78
00:06:34.835 --> 00:06:41.155
dentistry. In an effort to keep up with the demand for diamonds, synthetic diamonds have been developed.

79
00:06:41.915 --> 00:06:46.037
The majority of these synthetic diamonds are suitable only for industrial purposes.

80
00:06:46.918 --> 00:06:53.101
Only 10 percent of industrial-grade diamonds are natural diamonds; the other 90 percent are synthetic.

81
00:06:54.082 --> 00:06:56.783
Some artificial diamonds are also used for jewelry.

82
00:06:57.604 --> 00:07:02.066
Diamonds are known all over the world as precious stones that symbolize love and romance.

83
00:07:02.847 --> 00:07:05.088
But diamonds also have strength beneath their beauty.

84
00:07:05.905 --> 00:07:08.851
The diamond is much more than just another pretty face.
